A||||9||151 in. of love||02||||1||
Z||000000||151 in. of love||02-12-2001||07:45 PM||||OK.<BR>I have been discussing this with my friend.<BR>If an engine has a misfire, that means the 02 sensor will see more oxygen.<P>So, would the ECM provide more fuel because it thinks the engine is running lean.<BR>or<BR>Would it provide less fuel because it thinks that there is a rich mixture and not burning properly?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||02-12-2001||07:52 PM||||Actual responce of the O2 sensor can be pretty strange. Try digging around in the archives of <A HREF="http://www.motorage.com/" TARGET=_blank>Motor Age</A> and <A HREF="http://www.motor.com" TARGET=_blank>Motor Magazine</A>. They have a number of articles about it. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||02-12-2001||07:54 PM||||If the engine has a misfire the oxygen sensor will detect a rich mixture because of the unburnt fuel,tell the computer it's too rich and the computer will lean out the mixture. <BR>I had a 1985 Seville every once in a while it would lose power.I had a bad spark plug wire.When I changed the wires,no more problems.<p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 02-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||151 in. of love||02-14-2001||12:50 AM||||Hugh,<BR>So why do you say that about the O2?<BR>Could the loss of power only been the wire?<P>WOW OGRE!<BR>Those links are great. I found a lot of really good info. on there. Those definately made it into the favorites list.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||02-14-2001||06:55 AM||||The bad wire caused the cylinder to misfire once in a while.When it did the computer adjusted the mixture to compensate for the richer mixture and leaned out the engine,causing a pretty big loss of power.||||reg||1
Z||000005||CHUCKVH||02-14-2001||11:34 PM||||It depends on the type of misfire.  A misfire caused by an ignition problem will send unburnt fuel through the engine, causing a "rich" condition at the O2 sensor.  A misfire can also be caused by a fuel mixture that is too lean, so the O2 sensor will see a lean condition.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||02-15-2001||01:42 AM||||If the o2 sensor see's too much oxygen, it means the engine is running lean, because there's not enough fuel to burn with all the oxygen.  Exact opposite for a rich condition.  Too much fuel and not enough o2.||||reg||1
Z||000007||151 in. of love||02-15-2001||08:40 PM||||So,<BR>If I had a plug not firing,<BR>The O2 would see excessive oxygen-<BR>  According to standard, the ECM interprets this as a LEAN mixture. Henceforth, adding more fuel to the cylinders.<BR>  According to Hugh, the ECM interprets this as a RICH mixture, and leans out the fuel.<P>Any comments from the commentators?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||02-15-2001||08:43 PM||||Well, maybe somebody with a working scanner can get some readings and then pull a plug wire and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||02-15-2001||11:21 PM||||Cooter, dig in the motor archives at the links I put above. They did just what you asked. The results were pretty interesting.||||reg||1
